{"kind":"report","text":"Exact-head review of a62962aefbf018e3574b11ee2265dd3c92946e70, resting on all seven artifacts, packages/policy/README.md first. Changes requested for one design correction; the admission-order amendment is acceptable.\n\nArchitecture: R-ADM-1 keeps signature, idempotency, authority, schema, secrets, lane/lease, known head and bounded diff ahead of proposal policy. Moving refuse ahead of claim containment changes the explanation while retaining every admission invariant. Keep the configuration-recovery bypass and one shared refuse/require meter. The pack's updated measurements pass on both hosts. Lane A still has to implement this order and prove an actual outside-claim conflict proposal returns jj-conflicts; the policy-only corpus does not prove Room ordering.\n\nP2: the new rule confuses changed paths with conflict data remaining in the proposed tree (pack.ts jjConflicts; policy.json; policy-pack.md). It faithfully implements request 66d6fb14's literal changed-path criterion, but that criterion makes its own recommended repair fail. Starting with an imported or previously accepted canonical tree containing the root conflict directories, a clean child removes every .jjconflict-base/side directory. Those deletions remain in proposal.paths, so the rule calls the clean head unresolved and refuses it. An unrelated child can leave the directories untouched: only guide.md appears in the diff, so the rule passes despite the directories remaining in the head. Existing imported repositories and policy upgrades make these reachable.\n\nIndependent reproductions build a local Git repository with the documented directory representation, derive the clean-child deletion paths and the dirty-child unrelated paths using Git, and feed those facts into the actual policy evaluator. Both failures reproduce on Node and workerd. The deletion case supplies status:\"deleted\" in proposal.changed; the rule ignores it. These are constructed Git fixtures, not a live jj transport claim.\n\nCorrect the criterion and the user-facing claim together. A rule that detects newly introduced conflict paths must at least exclude deletions and rename source paths; call it that limited check rather than claiming the whole head is conflict-free. To retain the stated goal of refusing unresolved conflict trees, provide a bounded Room-owned fact about the proposed head's root entries and consume it in the policy rule. Cover importing an existing conflict tree, removing all conflict data, leaving it untouched, renaming it out, new conflict directories, ordinary nested names, recovery lanes and budget bounds. Route any input/ordering changes to A and the contract, rather than requiring users to disable this guard to resolve conflicts.\n\nSecurity: the earlier policy stage grants no new authority and must never bypass containment, admin obligations or recovery protections. A stable diagnostic is useful, but a passing delta test cannot certify the current tree.\n\nSimplification: retain one policy rule and one authoritative computation of the relevant head facts; avoid a second Room-specific refusal with a different explanation.\n\nValidation: clean root strict type check, 193 Node policy tests and 192 workerd tests passed with the existing one skip. Three additional independent cases passed on each host (two failure reproductions and the recovery bypass). All seven changed artifacts were reviewed against the approved main bases, including protocol, measurements and compiled demo parity. Diagnostics: /tmp/artroom-checker-jj-diagnostics/checker-jj-refuse.test.ts; copy beside pack.test.ts to rerun. Candidate worktree is clean. Official format source checked: https://docs.jj-vcs.dev/latest/git-compatibility/#format-mapping-details.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e87c7f959ba09030a139cff4c28fb35f7a916939","binding":"assigned","head":"a62962aefbf018e3574b11ee2265dd3c92946e70","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git